    "media:imagetext": "Mr. Bixby\n• - 2 - ,\nSeptember 22, 1937\nUndoubtedly the Generalissimo hoped for the maximum of time\npast two years he had any expectation of avoiding it by putting up any fake\nshow and final agreement which would involve any material expansion of the\nJapanese sphere of influence in China. In other words I do not think, as\nyou say, that the Chinese patience became exhausted, but rather that the\nJapanese, judging the situation correctly, forced the issue when they did\ninstead of giving the General any more time to prepare.\nwhat the Central Powers did in 1914 when France and Russia were preparing\n22323\nfor a showdown in 1916 or later.\nThere is considerable talk here and in the papers to the\neffect that the Chinese Government was dragged into this war through pres-\nsure of public opinion and inability to restrain the army leaders and the\nmasses of the people any longer. This theory does not fibe with the fact\nof the very complete and ample preparation which was made for this eventu-\nhave been controlled by the Government if that had been their policy. It\nseems more likely that in Japan the\npeople were dragged in by the army lead-\ners and this may have important repercussions later.\nI never could see why it was that there was such wide-spread\nopinion among the \"China hands\" that the next incident would not seriously\ninvolve Shanghai. There were two controlling reasons, to my mind, why the\nJapanese had to attack at Shanghai. One was the necessity of their protect-\ning their great number of nationals there from mob violence, and the second\nwas the important economic consequence to China of paralizing and control-\nling its most important commercial city. It was equally certain that the\nChinese would resist any military activity around Shanghai although every-\nbody, including the Japanese, miscalculated the vigor of the Chinese resist-\nance around that point.\nIn any case, however, it seemed to me, and I re-\nmember so telling Bond here in July, that far from being \u0026 safe haven, I\nthought the International Settlement might become perhaps the most dangerous\nplace to be in China. It must be only a question of time before antipathy\ntowards Japan will turn into antipathy towards all foreigners--if external\naid is not forthcoming.\nI am not going to be foolish enough to try to predict the\nfuture course of events other.\nthan to say that if my theories\nas above set\nforth are correct, it is a certainty that the Chinese resistance will be\nvigorous and long\ndrawn out. Much must depend on the extent of the military\nsupplies which they have been able to accumulate during the last two or three\nyears, and their ability to obtain new supplies in spite of the coastal block-\nade by Japan. Only time will show the answer to these questions and it seems\nto me that gasoline supplies will be a vital factor since, unless I am mistaken,\nthere is no gasoline production in China, and it would be very difficult at\npresent to bring this in except through channels which the Japanese will al-\nmost certainly be successful in blockading. However, experience has shown",
